The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```java
final HashSet<Integer> testSet = Sets.newHashSet();
testSet.add(System.identityHashCode(codec));
```
Reason:
The `testSet` is a shared resource accessed by multiple threads without synchronization, which can lead to race conditions. When multiple threads call `testSet.add()`, they might simultaneously read, modify, and write to the set, resulting in inconsistent states or incorrect size calculations.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
synchronized (testSet) {
    testSet.add(System.identityHashCode(codec));
}
```
Reason:
Adding the `synchronized` block ensures that only one thread can modify `testSet` at a time, preventing race conditions and ensuring that the integrity of the shared resource is maintained during concurrent access. This resolves the issue of inconsistent results when multiple threads try to add items to the set simultaneously.

